hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well  in book 5 of the City of God Augustine  of Hippo engages in a long and somewhat  complex discussion about the  compatibility between two seemingly  incompatible doctrines one of these is  the freedom of the human will or freedom  of choice  and the other is what we call divine  foreknowledge the idea that God would  know everything that is going to happen  from eternity or from the inception of  things and this was not a problem that  came up uniquely for Christian  philosophers and theologians as we see  it had already been discussed quite a  bit by ancient Greek and Roman  philosophers long before Christianity  came on the scene because it was felt to  be quite an issue and there are a range  of different ideas or doctrines on this  this convoluted question so the the core  issue here is you know if God exists and  God does have foreknowledge of  everything that is going to happen that  means God knows in advance is freedom of  will actually possible freedom of the  human will because it seems that if God  knows everything knows future  contingents as they're called or the  things that are going to happen if  they're if God has sort of a  propositional knowledge that I will at  this point walk across the room and  address you with these very words then  it seems like I don't actually have a  choice in it I have to do it it happens  behind us S&amp;E and you can take God out  of the picture if you like you can  substitute some sort of mechanistic  picture of the universe or you know any  other way of having something that we  could call fate or necessity ruling if  that's the case then it seems that we  don't have free will  and Augustine is going to place himself  here in conversation with Cicero a  earlier non-christian Roman philosopher  and statesman Cicero wrote a book called  on fate in which he discussed this very  problem he also discusses it to some  degree in on the nature of the gods and  on divination and Cicero opposed himself  to the Stoics on this matter the Stoics  were a school of philosophy that among  other things held that there is a God  which they called Zeus and understood as  this divine logos permeating everything  they thought that Zeus was fully  rational and also had a sort of  providential care for every single thing  within the universe so everything is set  up structured in certain ways and they  also held that everything is connected  together in some sort of causal Nexus so  the Stoics were willing to say that  there could be divine foreknowledge  that's why they thought there was  divination and Cicero is going to attack  them on this now the Stoics of course  didn't go all the way to saying  everything is totally necessitated they  said some things are not up to necessity  they are up to us but the kind of causes  that we are also within this vast causal  Nexus and it's you know you can  interpret their position in various ways  but one way of interpreting is we don't  really have free will and so Augustine  is having Cicero engaged with the Stoics  you know and say listen because there is  divine foreknowledge everything is fated  which means that we don't actually have  freewill we can either go along you know  kicking and screaming but we'll still  have to go along or we can agree to it  and follow along with it that much is at  least up to us Augustine takes Cicero as  developing a different alternate  position on this Cicero wanted to say  that there is no knowledge  of future events so there's no  foreknowledge either on the part of  human beings or on the part of God and  what this would mean is that we would in  fact have freedom of the will so Cicero  is denying one thing in order to keep  another thing which he sees is  incompatible Augustine says Cicero  doesn't really see that the consequence  of this is that he's essentially in the  same position as the fool in Psalms who  says there is no God the fool says in  his heart there is no God because to  deny foreknowledge is to deny that God  is in fact God a point that we'll come  back to momentarily so this is felt to  be a central problem on the part of  Augustine and Cicero is in effect  putting us in a dilemma he says he says  if the knowledge of future things is  granted there follows a chain of  consequences which ends in this there  can be nothing depending on our own free  wills if there is anything depending on  our wills we must go backwards by the  same step of reasoning till we arrive at  the conclusion there is no foreknowledge  how do we do this we go backward through  this if there is free will all things do  not happen according to fate if all  things do not happen according to fate  there is not a certain order of causes  if there is not a certain order of  causes neither is there a certain order  of things for known by God and if there  is no fixed in certain order of causes  for known by God all things cannot be  said to happen as he foreknew they would  happen and so there you know there is no  foreknowledge of future events in God so  we're placed in a dilemma here  according to Augustine by Cicero where  we either have to choose one or the  other and it's 